{"body": "I treat A (the P7 excerpt on criticism targets), B (the P9 excerpt on local alternatives, global availability, and the possible compatibility constraint), J (the construct occurrence), and the criticism of J (the criticize occurrence) as distinct historical occurrences. I also treat the earlier E009 occurrence recorded in the packet as a separate criticism artifact, not as warehouse evidence and not as an endorsement of J. The criticism of J establishes a localized defect, so I propose a successor only at that point. I do not overwrite J, and I do not claim that any preservation promise, syntax check, or prior success executes a repair.\n\n## Proposed successor J*\n\nI retain J’s five-layer target-indexed availability account: local alternatives L, aggregation A, constraint model C, claim strength R, and target register T. I also retain J’s central move that P9’s compatibility objection is claim-relative. What I amend is J’s first classification of the objection.\n\nThe amendment is to separate two things that J’s first formulation blurs:\n\n- the **object** of P9’s objection; and\n- the **P7 target category** of the criticism.\n\nP9’s wording fixes an object: intervals. P9 says that per-booking minima and maxima give an interval, but intervals lose correlations, because booking A’s high alternative and booking B’s high alternative might share an unnamed physical resource and be jointly impossible. The objection is therefore primarily to an interval’s use as a representation of joint feasibility. It is not, without more, an objection to computing the interval as a bound. A bound can be computed without assuming independence, and it can remain a valid over-approximation even when correlations exist.\n\nSo J’s sentence “It attacks A’s independence assumption” is too coarse. Independence is one possible diagnosis of why an interval was mistaken for something stronger, but P9 does not entail that the aggregation actually assumed independence. The sharper statement is:\n\n> P9’s compatibility objection attacks the interval’s sufficiency as a representation of joint feasibility. Whether that becomes a P7 criticism of an aggregation rule, a constraint model, a claim strength, a projection policy, or the snapshot as a whole depends on what the account claims.\n\nThis changes J’s target register T. T must now record both the object and the P7 target category. It is not enough to say that a criticism targets “an assumption” or “a rule.” One must also say what the criticism is about: an interval, a record, a supersession rule, a projection, a completeness claim, and so on. The object can remain fixed while the P7 target shifts with the account’s claim strength.\n\nThe amendment also makes the claim layer R more explicit. R must distinguish at least three strengths:\n\n1. **Bound claim**: true availability lies within the interval.\n2. **Feasibility claim**: every value in the interval is achievable.\n3. **Decision-grade claim**: the interval is sufficient for allocation or some other downstream decision.\n\nP9’s compatibility objection is decisive against the naive feasibility claim and against the decision-grade claim when correlations matter. It is not decisive against the bound claim. If the account says only “availability lies in [lo, hi],” then P9 does not refute it; it only shows that the interval may be looser than the feasible set. If the account says “every value in [lo, hi] is jointly realizable,” then P9 refutes that. If the account says “the interval is enough for allocation,” P9 targets the projection policy or the downstream use.\n\nThe rest of J remains. L still holds per-booking alternatives under the chosen reading of supersession, expiry, release, and ties. A still maps local alternatives to a global representation. C still lists the constraints actually modeled; capacity is one, and a shared physical resource is absent unless added. R now states claim strength more carefully. T now records object and target separately. J’s internal criticism also remains: exposing local alternatives per booking does not by itself expose cross-booking incompatibilities. A local-exposure remedy can still overclaim globally if its aggregation or claim layer pretends otherwise. J’s discriminating question also remains: does adding a resource constraint that leaves every booking’s local alternatives and per-booking bounds unchanged but removes some joint combinations change the global output? If yes, correlations are represented. If no, the output is an independent projection.\n\n## Why the criticism warrants this change\n\nThe criticism’s concrete case is useful. Capacity is 10. Booking A has local alternatives {2, 5}. Booking B has local alternatives {3, 4}. A shared resource makes the combination (5, 4) impossible, but the other combinations remain possible. Per-booking minima and maxima give A ∈ {2, 5}, B ∈ {3, 4}, sum in [5, 9], so availability in [1, 5]. The jointly realizable sums are 5, 6, and 8, giving availabilities 5, 4, and 2. The interval [1, 5] contains 1 and 3, which are not achievable. But computing [1, 5] did not require assuming independence. It merely failed to exclude the impossible combination. An account that says “availability lies in [1, 5]” is not refuted by P9. An account that says “availability can be any value in [1, 5]” is refuted. So J’s initial sentence conflates two different accounts.\n\nThat is a real defect in J’s first target classification. It is not a refutation of the whole target-indexed account. J’s later conditional reading already moves toward the correction: if the account claims that the min/max interval is the feasible range, then the objection targets the aggregation rule’s treatment of combinability. The successor makes that correction explicit and uses it to refine T and R.\n\n## Protected uses that remain\n\nThe following protected uses remain distinguishable:\n\n- Questioning a rule differs from questioning its records.\n- Accepting or rejecting a rule remains independent of accepting or rejecting data.\n- P9’s cross-booking incompatibility remains a conditional possibility, not an observation that a shared resource exists.\n- Local alternatives, aggregation, bounds, and correlation objections have different roles.\n- Exposing alternatives and objecting to downstream resolution remain disputed positions.\n- P7’s target taxonomy remains available: rule, record, booking history, snapshot, assumption.\n- J’s five-layer organization remains available, with T now carrying an object/target distinction and R now carrying a more explicit claim-strength distinction.\n\n## Language extension\n\nIf a formal carrier were desired, one could propose adding an object/target pair, a claim-strength field, a constraint model, and a compatibility relation. That would be a separate proposed object, not an installed part of the frozen carrier and not an amendment to A, B, or J. I do not install such an extension here. I also do not claim that any syntax check or preservation promise demonstrates executed repair or creativity.\n\n## What remains unresolved\n\nIt remains unresolved whether any shared resource actually exists, whether capacity is a scalar or a resource vector, and whether downstream decisions require a feasible set or only a conservative summary. It also remains unresolved whether the target of P9’s compatibility objection should be recorded as a missing resource model in the snapshot or as an overclaim in the aggregation or projection layer. J’s successor only prevents the initial misclassification: P9’s objection has a fixed object, namely the interval’s correlation loss, but its P7 target category depends on the account’s claim strength.", "commitments": "I treat A (the P7 excerpt on criticism targets), B (the P9 excerpt on local alternatives, global availability, and the possible compatibility constraint), J (the construct occurrence), and the criticism of J (the criticize occurrence) as distinct historic